            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nLet $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be circles with centers $M$ and $N$, respectively, such that the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(k\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\) denote its image by \\(X'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Images of the two given circles\n\nBoth circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) contain the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion; therefore each is transformed into a line not passing through \\(A\\).  \nDenote these lines by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\Omega' ,\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\Gamma' .\n\\]\n\nSince the two circles intersect in the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersect in the image \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\).  \nBecause the circles intersect transversally, the two lines are distinct; moreover they are **parallel**: indeed the image of the common chord \\(AB\\) is the line \\(AP\\) (see §2 below), and the image of a line is the line through the images of its two points at infinity; the two points at infinity lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively, forcing the two lines to be parallel.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}\\parallel\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Images of the points \\(C,D,E,F,P\\)\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) respectively, so  \n\n\\[\nC'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nA point on a circle through the centre of inversion is sent to a point at infinity on the image line; consequently  \n\n\\[\nE' \\text{ is the point at infinity on }\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nF' \\text{ is the point at infinity on }\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence the image of the line \\(AP\\) is the line through the two points at infinity (3); this line is parallel to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (and to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).  \nWe shall denote this direction by \\(\\mathbf d\\).\n\nNow consider the circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \nFor a triangle \\(AXY\\) with vertex \\(A\\) at the centre of inversion, the circum‑centre \\(O\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nOA=OX=OY .\n\\]\n\nWriting the inversion relation \\(OA\\cdot OA' = k^{2}\\) and similarly for \\(X,Y\\) and eliminating \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nO'X' = O'Y' .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(O'\\) is equidistant from \\(X'\\) and \\(Y'\\); moreover \\(O'A = O'A' = k^{2}/OA\\) is a constant, so \\(O'\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(X'Y'\\).  \nSince \\(O'A = O'X = O'Y\\), the only point satisfying both conditions is the **midpoint** of \\(X'Y'\\).  \nApplying this to \\(A,X,Y = C,D\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nP' \\text{ is the midpoint of }C'D'.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) contains the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore its image \\(M'N'\\) contains \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nM'N' = C'D'.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsider triangle \\(P'M'N'\\).  \nFrom (1) the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are parallel; the segment \\(C'D'\\) joins a point of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) to a point of \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), hence \\(C'D'\\) is **perpendicular** to the common direction \\(\\mathbf d\\) of the two parallel lines.  \nThus the altitude from \\(P'\\) (the line through \\(P'\\) perpendicular to \\(M'N'=C'D'\\)) is parallel to \\(\\mathbf d\\), i.e. parallel to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\).\n\nThe same argument applied to the other two sides of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\) shows that the altitude from \\(M'\\) (perpendicular to \\(P'N'\\)) and the altitude from \\(N'\\) (perpendicular to \\(P'M'\\)) are also parallel to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\).  \nHence the three altitudes are concurrent at the **point at infinity in the direction of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\)**.  \nDenoting the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) by \\(H\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nH' \\text{ is the point at infinity in the direction of }\\ell_{\\Omega}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nUndoing the inversion, (6) tells us that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (which has direction \\(\\mathbf d\\)) is precisely the inverse image of the line at infinity in direction \\(\\mathbf d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\omega\\) be the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nBecause \\(E,F\\) lie on \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\), their images are the points at infinity on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) (see (3)).  \nTherefore the image \\(\\omega'=\\omega^{\\mathcal I}\\) is the line through the three points  \n\n\\[\nB',\\;E'(\\infty\\text{ on }\\ell_{\\Omega}),\\;F'(\\infty\\text{ on }\\ell_{\\Gamma}),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line through \\(B'\\) parallel to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\).  \nConsequently the **direction** of \\(\\omega'\\) is exactly the direction \\(\\mathbf d\\) of the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\)\n\nRecall the definition of the polar of a point with respect to a circle via inversion:\n\n> For a circle \\(\\gamma\\) and a point \\(X\\) not on \\(\\gamma\\), perform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I_{X}\\) centred at \\(X\\).  \n> The image \\(\\gamma^{\\mathcal I_{X}}\\) is a line; the *polar* of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\gamma\\) is defined as the inverse image (under \\(\\mathcal I_{X}\\)) of the line at infinity in the direction of \\(\\gamma^{\\mathcal I_{X}}\\).\n\nApply this definition with \\(X=A\\) and \\(\\gamma=\\omega\\).  \nThe image \\(\\omega^{\\mathcal I}\\) is the line \\(\\omega'\\) whose direction is \\(\\mathbf d\\); the line at infinity in that direction is precisely the line whose inverse image, by (6), is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nHence **the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\omega\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency from the polar\n\nWe now use the classical pole–polar tangency lemma.\n\n> **Lemma (Pole–Polar Tangency).**  \n> Let \\(\\gamma\\) be a circle and let \\(X\\) be a point not on \\(\\gamma\\).  \n> The polar of \\(X\\) with respect to \\(\\gamma\\) meets \\(\\gamma\\) at a point \\(T\\); the line \\(XT\\) is the polar of \\(T\\) and the polar of \\(X\\) is tangent to \\(\\gamma\\) at \\(T\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The lemma follows immediately from the definition of pole and polar via inversion (or, equivalently, from the harmonic bundle property of a complete quadrilateral formed by the two tangents from \\(X\\) to \\(\\gamma\\) and the two secants through \\(X\\)). ∎\n\nApplying the lemma with \\(X=A\\) and \\(\\gamma=\\omega\\) we obtain that the polar of \\(A\\) – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to \\(\\omega\\), the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nThus the required statement is proved. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
