ersion, a point distinct from the centre maps to a image line; it is **not** sent to infinity. The only point that maps to infinity is the centre \\(A\\) itself. Hence the claim that \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\) are points at infinity is false.\n\n**Quote (third part):**  \n> \u201cNow consider the circum\u2011centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\). \u2026 Thus \\(O'\\) is equidistant from \\(X'\\) and \\(Y'\\); \u2026 the only point satisfying both conditions is the **midpoint** of \\(X'Y'\\). Applying this to \\(A,X,Y = C,D\\) we obtain \\(P'\\) is the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The reasoning misapplies the inversion formula. For the circumcenter \\(P\\) we have \\(PA = PC = PD\\). Using \\(PA\\cdot PA' = k^{2}\\) and similarly for \\(PC,PD\\) gives \\(PA' = PC' = PD'\\). Thus **\\(P'\\) is equidistant from \\(A', C', D'\\)**, not merely from \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\). Since \\(A'\\) is the point at infinity (the image of the centre), the condition \u201c\\(P'\\) is equidistant from a finite point and a point at infinity\u201d forces \\(P'\\) to lie on a line **parallel** to the direction of the line through \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\), not at the midpoint. Therefore the conclusion that \\(P'\\) is the midpoint of \\(C'D'\\) is **false**.\n\n**Conclusion:** Both statements about \\(E',F'\\) and about \\(P'\\) are **Critical Errors**.\n\n---\n\n### Section\u202f3 \u2013 The image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\n**Quote:**  \n> \u201cThe line \\(MN\\) contains the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); therefore its image \\(M'N'\\) contains \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\). Consequently \\(M'N' = C'D'\\).\u201d\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The image of a line **through** the inversion centre is the same line, so \\(MN\\) maps to itself, not to a new line \\(M'N'\\). Moreover, even if we accept that the image line passes through \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\), the identification \\(M'N' = C'D'\\) presumes that the image of \\(MN\\) coincides with the segment joining the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\