\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous proof of the statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   Invert the whole configuration about a circle \\(\\mathcal I\\) centred at \\(A\\).  \n   Because the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, they are transformed into two *parallel* lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   Consequently the second intersections \\(E,F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) are sent to the points at infinity on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); the image of the line \\(AP\\) is therefore the line parallel to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (and to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).  \n\n2. **Images of the basic points.**  \n   Denote by a prime the image after inversion.  \n   \\[\n   C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\qquad \n   P' \\text{ is the midpoint of }C'D'.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]  \n   (1) follows from the elementary fact that the circum‑centre of a triangle having the centre of inversion as a vertex is mapped to the midpoint of the opposite side.\n\n3. **The orthocentre \\(H\\).**  \n   The line \\(MN\\) is sent to the line \\(M'N'\\) which passes through \\(C'\\) and \\(D'\\); hence \\(M'N'=C'D'\\).  \n   In triangle \\(P'M'N'\\) the altitude from \\(P'\\) is the line through \\(P'\\) perpendicular to \\(C'D'\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\parallel\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and \\(C'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},D'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), the segment \\(C'D'\\) is *perpendicular* to the common direction of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   Therefore the altitude from \\(P'\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega