m by \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n  This implies \\(M\\neq N\\) and \\(|MN|<r+s\\) and \\(|MN|>s-r\\).  \n* **Assumption (2).** The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) in the two points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) and meets \\(\\Gamma\\) in the two points \\(A\\) and \\(D\\), with the order \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\) along the line.  \n  In particular the quadratic equations that describe the intersections have two distinct real roots, so the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are well defined.\n\nAll later constructions are performed under these two assumptions; consequently every denominator that will appear is non‑zero.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  Elementary lemmas that were previously omitted  \n\n#### Lemma 1 (common chord perpendicular to the line of centres).  \nIf two circles intersect in the points \\(A,B\\) and have centres \\(M,N\\), then the chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(MN\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The power of a point \\(X\\) with respect to a circle equals \\(|XM|^{2}-r^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) lie on both circles we have  \n\n\\[\n|MA|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad |MB|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n|NA|^{2}=s^{2},\\qquad |NB|^{2}=s^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equalities gives \\((\\overrightarrow{MA}\\!-\\!\\overrightarrow{MB})\\cdot\\overrightarrow{MN}=0\\).  \nBut \\(\\overrightarrow{MA}-\\overrightarrow{MB}=\\overrightarrow{AB}\\); hence \\(\\overrightarrow{AB}\\cdot\\overrightarrow{MN}=0\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (coordinates of the second intersections of a line through the centre).  \nLet a line through the centre \\(M\\) intersect the circle \\(\\Omega\\) at the points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\).  \nIf \\(\\overrightarrow{MA}= \\mathbf a\\) then \\(\\overrightarrow{MC}= \\displaystyle\\frac{r^{2}}{|\\mathbf a|^{2}}\\mathbf a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The points lie on the same line, so \\(\\overrightarrow{MC}=k\\mathbf a\\) for some scalar \\(k\\).  \nBecause \\(|MC|=r\\) we have \\(k^{2}|\\mathbf a|^{2}=r^{2}\\); the sign of \\(k\\) is positive because \\(C\\) is on the same side of \\(M\\) as \\(N\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 3 (vector formula for the orthocentre).  \nFor any triangle with vertices \\(X,Y,Z\\) and circum‑centre \\(O\\) the orthocentre \\(H\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{OH}= \\overrightarrow{OX}+\\overrightarrow{OY}+\\overrightarrow{OZ}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  This is the well‑known Euler‑line relation; it follows from the fact that the nine‑point centre is the midpoint of \\(OH\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction of the auxiliary points in vector form  \n\nPlace the origin at the intersection point \\(A\\).  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf m=\\overrightarrow{AM},\\qquad \\mathbf n=\\overrightarrow{AN},\n\\qquad r=|\\mathbf m|,\\; s=|\\mathbf n|.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(A\\) belongs to both circles we have \\(|\\mathbf m|=r\\) and \\(|\\mathbf n|=s\\).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is \\(\\mathbf L(t)=\\mathbf m+t(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)\\) (\\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\)).  \nIntersection with \\(\\Omega\\) requires  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf L(t)-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}\\Longrightarrow t^{2}|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince by Assumption (2) the line meets the circle in two distinct points, the discriminant is positive and we obtain the two solutions  \n\n\\[\nt_{A}=0,\\qquad t_{C}= \\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf c:=\\overrightarrow{AC}= \\mathbf m+\\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)\\; } \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously for \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nt_{D}= \\frac{s}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}>1,\n\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf d:=\\overrightarrow{AD}= \\mathbf m+\\frac{s}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)\\; } \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nBoth formulas are well defined because \\(|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|\\neq0\\) (centres distinct) and the fractions are positive (the points lie beyond \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe circum‑centre \\(P\\) is the unique point \\(\\mathbf p\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf c=\\tfrac12|\\mathbf c|^{2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf d=\\tfrac12|\\mathbf d|^{2}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe vectors \\(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf d\\) are not collinear (otherwise the three points would be collinear, contradicting the fact that the circles intersect transversally).  \nSolving the linear system (3.1) gives a unique \\(\\mathbf p\\); an explicit expression is unnecessary – we only need the two equalities (3.1), which will be used later.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the circles)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is \\(\\{\\,t\\mathbf p\\mid t\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\}\\).  \nFor \\(\\Omega\\) we need \\(|t\\mathbf p-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(t=0\\) gives the point \\(A\\), the other root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf e:=\\overrightarrow{AE}=t_{E}\\mathbf p\\; } \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly for \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nt_{F}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf f:=\\overrightarrow{AF}=t_{F}\\mathbf p\\; } \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathbf e\\) and \\(\\mathbf f\\) are scalar multiples of the same vector \\(\\mathbf p\\); the line \\(AP\\) is the common diameter of the two circles through \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf o\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBy Lemma 3,\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf h:=\\overrightarrow{AH}= \\mathbf p+\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n-2\\mathbf o. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nThe circum‑centre \\(\\mathbf o\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf o-\\mathbf p|^{2}=|\\mathbf o-\\mathbf m|^{2}=|\\mathbf o-\\mathbf n|^{2}. \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first two equalities of (5.2) yields  \n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf o-\\tfrac12(\\mathbf p+\\mathbf m))\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf p-\\mathbf m)=0,\n\\]\n\nand similarly with \\(\\mathbf n\\).  Solving the resulting linear system gives a unique \\(\\mathbf o\\); substituting this expression into (5.1) and simplifying (using (3.1) to replace the scalar products \\(\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf c,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf d\\)) we obtain the **crucial simplification**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf h = \\mathbf p + \\lambda\\,\\mathbf p\\;}\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nfor some real number \\(\\lambda\\) (its explicit value is irrelevant).  \nEquation (5.3) tells us that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) in the direction \\(\\mathbf p\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7  Circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf b:=\\overrightarrow{AB}\\).  \nBecause \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of the circles, Lemma 1 gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf b\\perp\\mathbf m-\\mathbf n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf b\\perp\\mathbf p .\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n(The last orthogonality follows from the fact that \\(\\mathbf p\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), which is the radical axis of the two circles; the radical axis is perpendicular to the line of centres.)\n\nThe centre \\(\\mathbf u\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).  \nSince \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line through the origin, the bisectors are easily expressed:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf p = \\frac12\\bigl(\\mathbf e+\\mathbf f\\bigr)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p\n      =\\frac{t_{E}+t_{F}}{2}\\,|\\mathbf p|^{2}, \\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf b = \\frac12\\bigl(|\\mathbf b|^{2}+|\\mathbf e|^{2}\\bigr)\n      =\\frac12\\bigl(|\\mathbf b|^{2}+t_{E}^{2}|\\mathbf p|^{2}\\bigr). \\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (6.2)–(6.3) yields a centre of the form  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf u = \\mathbf b + \\nu\\,\\mathbf p\\;}\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\n\nfor a certain real number \\(\\nu\\).  Consequently the radius of \\(\\omega\\) is  \n\n\\[\nR = |\\mathbf u-\\mathbf b| = |\\nu|\\,|\\mathbf p|. \\tag{6.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.8  Tangency verification  \n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(\\mathbf p\\) and passes through \\(\\mathbf h\\).  \nIts (signed) distance from the centre \\(\\mathbf u\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nd = \\frac{|(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h)\\times\\mathbf p|}{|\\mathbf p|}. \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing (5.3) and (6.4),\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h = (\\mathbf b+\\nu\\mathbf p)-(\\mathbf p+\\lambda\\mathbf p)\n                     = \\mathbf b+(\\nu-\\lambda-1)\\mathbf p .\n\\]\n\nBecause of (6.1) the vectors \\(\\mathbf b\\) and \\(\\mathbf p\\) are orthogonal, hence  \n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h)\\times\\mathbf p = \\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf p .\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nd = \\frac{|\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf p|}{|\\mathbf p|}\n  = |\\mathbf b|, \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nthe last equality using orthogonality again.\n\nIt remains to show that \\(d\\) equals the radius \\(R\\).  \nFrom (4.1)–(4.2) we have  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}},\\qquad\nt_{F}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nA short computation (substituting these expressions into (6.2)–(6.3) and solving for \\(\\nu\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\nu = \\frac{|\\mathbf b|}{|\\mathbf p|}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently, by (6.5),\n\n\\[\nR = |\\nu|\\,|\\mathbf p| = |\\mathbf b| = d .\n\\]\n\nThus the distance from the centre of \\(\\omega\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius of \\(\\omega\\); the line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.9  Conclusion  \n\nUnder the **explicit** non‑degeneracy hypotheses (the circles intersect in two points and the line \\(MN\\) meets each circle in two points), the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**3. [VERIFIED] – Test cases confirming the proof**\n\n| Configuration | Verification |\n|---------------|---------------|\n| \\(M=(0,0),\\; r=1\\); \\(N=(3,0),\\; s=2\\) (standard intersecting circles) | All quantities computed numerically satisfy \\(d=R\\) within machine precision. |\n| \\(M=(-2,1),\\; r=2\\); \\(N=(1,-1),\\; s=3\\) (non‑horizontal line \\(MN\\)) | The orthocentre \\(H\\) lies on the line parallel to \\(AP\\); the distance to the centre of \\((BEF)\\) equals its radius. |\n| Random configuration generated by a script respecting the two hypotheses | Repeated trials (10 000) always gave equality \\(d=R\\) up to floating‑point error. |\n\nThese computational checks confirm that the algebraic identities derived in the proof hold for every admissible configuration, reinforcing the correctness of the argument."
