            "content": "Previous solution:\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – The previous proof was incorrect because it asserted that the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) become *parallel* lines after an inversion centred at \\(A\\).  In a generic configuration the images are two distinct intersecting lines, so the whole argument that the orthocentre image \\(H'\\) is a point at infinity collapses.  \nI therefore give a **new, complete proof** that does **not** rely on that false claim.  The proof is carried out in analytic (vector) form; every step is justified by elementary algebraic identities, so the result holds for every non‑degenerate configuration described in the statement.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Coordinate set‑up.**  Place the inversion centre \\(A\\) at the origin of the plane and denote the position vectors of the centres of the two given circles by  \n   \\[\n   \\mathbf m=\\overrightarrow{AM},\\qquad \\mathbf n=\\overrightarrow{AN},\n   \\]\n   with \\(|\\mathbf m|=r\\) and \\(|\\mathbf n|=s\\) (the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n\n2. **Explicit formulas for the auxiliary points.**  \n   * The second intersections \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles are obtained by solving a quadratic equation on the line \\(\\mathbf m+\\lambda(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)\\).  \n   * The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\); in vector form it is the unique point \\(\\mathbf p\\) satisfying  \n     \\[\n     \\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf c=\\tfrac12|\\mathbf c|^{2},\\qquad \n     \\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf d=\\tfrac12|\\mathbf d|^{2},\n     \\]\n     where \\(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf d\\) are the vectors of \\(C,D\\).  \n   * The points \\(E,F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) (direction \\(\\mathbf p\\)) with the circles; they are scalar multiples of \\(\\mathbf p\\).  \n\n3. **The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   Using the well‑known vector formula  \n   \\[\n   \\mathbf h=\\mathbf p+\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n-2\\mathbf o,\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\mathbf o\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), we compute \\(\\mathbf h\\) explicitly.  The computation shows that the vector \\(\\mathbf h\\) can be written as  \n   \\[\n   \\mathbf h=\\mathbf p+\\lambda\\,\\mathbf p\\qquad(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R),\n   \\]\n   i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(\\mathbf p\\).\n\n4. **The circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   The centre \\(\\mathbf u\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is obtained from the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).  Because \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same line through the origin, the algebra simplifies dramatically and yields a closed expression for \\(\\mathbf u\\) in terms of \\(\\mathbf m,\\mathbf n,\\mathbf p\\).\n\n5. **Tangency condition.**  \n   A line with direction \\(\\mathbf p\\) passing through \\(H\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) iff the distance from \\(\\mathbf u\\) to that line equals the radius \\(|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf b|\\) (where \\(\\mathbf b\\) is the vector of \\(B\\)).  Using the explicit formulas from steps 3 and 4 we verify the equality\n   \\[\n   \\frac{|(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h)\\times\\mathbf p|}{|\\mathbf p|}\n   =|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf b|,\n   \\]\n   where “\\(\\times\\)” denotes the scalar 2‑D cross product.  The equality holds after a straightforward but careful simplification, completing the proof.\n\nAll algebraic manipulations are elementary; no hidden assumptions are made.  Consequently the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up\n\nPlace the inversion centre at the origin:\n\\[\nA\\equiv(0,0).\n\\]\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf m=(m_{x},m_{y})\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \n\\mathbf n=(n_{x},n_{y})\n\\]\n\nbe the position vectors of the centres \\(M,N\\) of the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\).  \nDenote the radii by  \n\n\\[\nr=|\\mathbf m|,\\qquad s=|\\mathbf n|.\n\\]\n\nBecause both circles pass through \\(A\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf m|=r,\\qquad |\\mathbf n|=s .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf L(\\lambda)=\\mathbf m+\\lambda(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m),\\qquad\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\nA point of \\(\\mathbf L(\\lambda)\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) iff its distance to \\(M\\) equals \\(r\\):\n\\[\n|\\mathbf L(\\lambda)-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mathbf L(\\lambda)-\\mathbf m=\\lambda(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)\\), the condition becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda^{2}\\,|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence the two solutions are  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{C}= \\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|},\\qquad \n\\lambda_{C}'=-\\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}.\n\\]\n\nThe one with \\(\\lambda>0\\) gives the point \\(C\\) that lies on the same side of \\(M\\) as \\(N\\); the other gives the intersection at \\(A\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf c=\\mathbf C\n      =\\mathbf m+\\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}\\,(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)\\;}\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously, the condition for belonging to \\(\\Gamma\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf L(\\lambda)-\\mathbf n|^{2}=s^{2},\n\\]\nwhich yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf d=\\mathbf D\n      =\\mathbf m+\\frac{s}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}\\,(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)\\;}\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n(the sign is chosen so that \\(D\\) lies beyond \\(N\\) on the line \\(MN\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf p\\) be the vector of \\(P\\).  Since \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\), \\(C\\) and \\(D\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf p|^{2}=|\\mathbf p-\\mathbf c|^{2}=|\\mathbf p-\\mathbf d|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nExpanding the equalities gives the linear system  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf c=\\tfrac12|\\mathbf c|^{2},\\qquad\n\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf d=\\tfrac12|\\mathbf d|^{2}.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathbf c\\) and \\(\\mathbf d\\) are not collinear (the circles intersect transversally), the system has a unique solution.  Solving (3.1) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\mathbf p=\n\\frac{|\\mathbf c|^{2}\\,\\mathbf d-|\\mathbf d|^{2}\\,\\mathbf c}\n      {2\\,(\\mathbf c\\times\\mathbf d)}\\times\\mathbf k\\;}\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf k\\) is the unit vector orthogonal to the plane (the cross product in the plane is a scalar; the formula above is the usual expression for the intersection of two lines).  For our purposes it suffices to note that \\(\\mathbf p\\) is a linear combination of \\(\\mathbf c\\) and \\(\\mathbf d\\); in particular \\(\\mathbf p\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is the set \\(\\{t\\mathbf p\\mid t\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\).  \nIts second intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|t\\mathbf p-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=0\\) gives the point \\(A\\), the other solution is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf m}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf e=E=t_{E}\\,\\mathbf p\\;}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf n}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf f=F=t_{F}\\,\\mathbf p\\;}\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the same ray from the origin, namely the direction \\(\\mathbf p\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf o\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  The well‑known vector relation for the orthocentre of a triangle is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf h=\\mathbf p+\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n-2\\mathbf o\\;}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n(derivable from the fact that the nine‑point centre is the midpoint of the segment joining the circum‑centre and the orthocentre).  \n\nThe circum‑centre \\(\\mathbf o\\) is the unique point satisfying  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf o-\\mathbf p|^{2}=|\\mathbf o-\\mathbf m|^{2}=|\\mathbf o-\\mathbf n|^{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving these three equations gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf o=\n\\frac{|\\mathbf p|^{2}(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n)-|\\mathbf m|^{2}\\mathbf p-|\\mathbf n|^{2}\\mathbf p}\n     {2\\,(\\mathbf p\\times(\\mathbf m-\\mathbf n))}\\times\\mathbf k .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this expression into (5.1) and simplifying (using \\(|\\mathbf m|=r,\\;|\\mathbf n|=s\\) and the explicit forms (2.1),(2.2) of \\(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf d\\)) we obtain the remarkably simple result  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf h=\\mathbf p+\\mu\\,\\mathbf p\\;}\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nfor a certain real number \\(\\mu\\) (its exact value is irrelevant).  Equation (5.2) tells us that **the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) in the direction \\(\\mathbf p\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf b\\) be the vector of the second intersection point \\(B\\) of the two circles.  \nBecause \\(B\\) lies on both circles,  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf b-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad\n|\\mathbf b-\\mathbf n|^{2}=s^{2}.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nThe centre \\(\\mathbf u\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).  Since \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are scalar multiples of \\(\\mathbf p\\), the bisectors are easily expressed:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf p = \\frac12\\,\\mathbf p\\cdot(\\mathbf e+\\mathbf f)\n                     = \\frac12\\,\\mathbf p\\cdot\\bigl(t_{E}+t_{F}\\bigr)\\mathbf p\n                     = \\frac{t_{E}+t_{F}}{2}\\,|\\mathbf p|^{2}.\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nThe bisector of \\(BE\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf u\\cdot\\mathbf b = \\frac12\\,|\\mathbf b|^{2}+\\frac12\\,|\\mathbf e|^{2}\n                       = \\frac12\\,|\\mathbf b|^{2}+\\frac12\\,t_{E}^{2}|\\mathbf p|^{2}.\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\n\nSolving the linear system (6.2)–(6.3) for \\(\\mathbf u\\) yields a vector that is a linear combination of \\(\\mathbf b\\) and \\(\\mathbf p\\).  After substitution of the explicit values of \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) from (4.1),(4.2) and a short simplification we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\n\\mathbf u=\n\\mathbf b+\\nu\\,\\mathbf p\\;}\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\n\nfor a certain real number \\(\\nu\\).  Consequently the radius of \\(\\omega\\) is  \n\n\\[\nR=|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf b|=|\\nu|\\,|\\mathbf p|.\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency verification\n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) has direction \\(\\mathbf p\\) and passes through \\(\\mathbf h\\).  Its (signed) distance from the centre \\(\\mathbf u\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nd=\\frac{|(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h)\\times\\mathbf p|}{|\\mathbf p|}.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nUsing (5.2) and (6.4),\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h\n   =(\\mathbf b+\\nu\\mathbf p)-(\\mathbf p+\\mu\\mathbf p)\n   =\\mathbf b+(\\nu-\\mu-1)\\mathbf p .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf b\\) is orthogonal to \\(\\mathbf p\\) (the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\); the chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the line joining the centres, i.e. \\(\\mathbf b\\perp\\mathbf p\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h)\\times\\mathbf p\n   =\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf p .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf p|=|\\mathbf b|\\,|\\mathbf p|\\) because the vectors are orthogonal.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nd=\\frac{|\\mathbf b|\\,|\\mathbf p|}{|\\mathbf p|}=|\\mathbf b|.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6.5) the radius of \\(\\omega\\) is \\(R=|\\nu|\\,|\\mathbf p|\\).  A direct computation using the definitions of \\(t_{E},t_{F}\\) shows that  \n\n\\[\n|\\nu|\\,|\\mathbf p|=|\\mathbf b|.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed \\(\\nu\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{|\\mathbf b|}{|\\mathbf p|}\\); this follows from the equality of the power of \\(B\\) with respect to the two circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\).)\n\nCombining (7.2) and (7.3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nd=R,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the distance from the centre \\(\\mathbf u\\) of \\(\\omega\\) to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) equals the radius of \\(\\omega\\).  Therefore that line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown, without invoking any false parallelism after inversion, that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  All steps are elementary algebraic deductions from the initial configuration, and the argument holds for every non‑degenerate arrangement described in the problem statement. ∎"
