\\lambda)-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow \\lambda^{2}|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{C}= \\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|},\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf c=\\mathbf C\n      =\\mathbf m+\\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}\\,(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{D}= \\frac{s}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|},\n\\qquad \n\\mathbf d=\\mathbf D\n      =\\mathbf m+\\frac{s}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}\\,(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m).\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n*Circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).*\n\nSince \\(A\\equiv O\\), the circum‑centre \\(\\mathbf p\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf c=\\frac{|\\mathbf c|^{2}}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p\\cdot\\mathbf d=\\frac{|\\mathbf d|^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf d\\) are not collinear, (2.3) determines a unique vector \\(\\mathbf p\\).\n\n*Second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the circles.*\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is the ray \\(\\{t\\mathbf p\\mid t\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\).\nFor the circle \\(\\Omega\\),\n\n\\[\n|t\\mathbf p-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}\n\\Longrightarrow t^{2}|\\mathbf p|^{2}-2t\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m=0 .\n\\]\n\nThe non‑zero solution is  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{E}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}},\\qquad\n\\mathbf e=E=\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf p .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously for \\(\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{F}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}},\\qquad\n\\mathbf f=F=\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf p .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf o\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nFor any triangle, the orthocentre satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf h=\\mathbf p+\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n-2\\mathbf o\\;}\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n(derived from the fact that the nine‑point centre is the midpoint of the segment joining the circum‑centre and the orthocentre).\n\nThe circum‑centre \\(\\mathbf o\\) is characterised by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf o\\cdot\\mathbf p=\\frac{|\\mathbf p|^{2}}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf o\\cdot\\mathbf m=\\frac{|\\mathbf m|^{2}}{2}= \\frac{r^{2}}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf o\\cdot\\mathbf n=\\frac{|\\mathbf n|^{2}}{2}= \\frac{s^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nTaking the scalar product of (3.1) with \\(\\mathbf p\\) and using (3.2) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf h\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p\n   =|\\mathbf p|^{2}+ \\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m+\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n\n     -2\\!\\left(\\frac{|\\mathbf p|^{2}}{2}\\right)\n   =\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n).\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf h\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n)\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Circum‑centre \\(U\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf b\\) be the vector of the second intersection point \\(B\\) of the two circles.\nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\), write  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf e=\\lambda_{E}\\mathbf p,\\qquad\n\\mathbf f=\\lambda_{F}\\mathbf p .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe circum‑centre \\(\\mathbf u\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) satisfies the equal‑distance conditions  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf e|^{2}=|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf f|^{2},\n\\qquad\n|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf b|^{2}=|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf e|^{2}.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nThe first equality yields  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf u\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf e-\\mathbf f)=\\frac{|\\mathbf e|^{2}-|\\mathbf f|^{2}}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\mathbf u\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p\n   =\\frac{\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F}}{2}\\,|\\mathbf p|^{2}.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (2.4)–(2.5) for \\(\\lambda_{E},\\lambda_{F}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{E}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}},\\qquad\n\\lambda_{F}= \\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F}}{2}\\,|\\mathbf p|^{2}\n   =\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m+\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf u\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n)\\; } .\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Tangency\n\nFrom (3.3) and (4.4) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=0 .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nThus the vector \\(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h\\) is orthogonal to the direction \\(\\mathbf p\\) of the line \\(AP\\).  \nTherefore the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the segment \\(U\\!-\\!H\\); equivalently, it is perpendicular to the radius \\(U\\!-\\!B\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nA line through a point whose distance to the centre of a circle equals the radius is tangent to that circle.  \nBecause (5.1) gives \\(\\operatorname{dist}(U,\\; \\text{line }H\\parallel AP)=|U-B|\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point – it is tangent.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
