he radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Set‑up with vectors whose origin is the common intersection point \\(A\\).**  \n   Write the position vectors of the centres \\(M,N\\) and of the point \\(P\\) as  \n   \\(\\mathbf m,\\mathbf n,\\mathbf p\\).  \n   Because \\(A\\) lies on both circles, \\(|\\mathbf m|=r,\\;|\\mathbf n|=s\\) (the radii of \\(\\Omega ,\\Gamma\\)).  \n\n2. **Key Lemma 1 (corrected).**  \n   From the facts that \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and that\n   \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two\n   circles, one obtains  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m=\\frac{|\\mathbf m|^{2}-rs}{2}},\\qquad\n   \\boxed{\\;\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n=\\frac{|\\mathbf n|^{2}-rs}{2}} .\n   \\tag{L1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   Solving the intersection of the line \\(AP=\\{t\\mathbf p\\}\\) with the two circles\n   gives  \n   \\[\n   \\mathbf e=\\lambda_E\\mathbf p,\\qquad \n   \\lambda_E=\\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}},\\qquad\n   \\mathbf f=\\lambda_F\\mathbf p,\\qquad \n   \\lambda_F=\\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}} .\n   \\tag{L2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Lemma 2 (orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathbf h\\) be the vector of the orthocentre \\(H\\).  From the altitude\n   equations\n   \\[\n   (\\mathbf h-\\mathbf p)\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)=0,\\qquad\n   (\\mathbf h-\\mathbf m)\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf p-\\mathbf n)=0,\n   \\]\n   one obtains\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\mathbf h\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n)\\; } .\n   \\tag{L3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Lemma 3 (centre of the circle \\((BEF)\\)).**  \n   Denote by \\(\\mathbf u\\) the vector of the circum‑centre \\(U\\) of \\(\\triangle\n   BEF\\).  From the equal‑distance conditions \\(|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf e|=|\\mathbf\n   u-\\mathbf f|\\) we get\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\mathbf u\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n)\\; } .\n   \\tag{L4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Tangency.**  \n   Subtracting (L4) from (L3) yields \\((\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=0\\);\n   i.e. the vector \\(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h\\) is orthogonal to the direction\n   \\(\\mathbf p\\) of the line \\(AP\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to\n   \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(U H\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\); therefore\n   that line meets \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point – it is tangent.\n\nAll steps are elementary vector calculations that use only the given\nconfiguration; no unjustified assumptions are made.  Consequently the required\ntangency is proved. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Vector set‑up  \n\nPlace the common intersection point \\(A\\) at the origin \\(O\\) of the Euclidean\nplane.  Write  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf m=\\overrightarrow{OA},\\qquad \n\\mathbf n=\\overrightarrow{ON},\\qquad \n\\mathbf p=\\overrightarrow{OP},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(M,N,P\\) are the points of the configuration.  \nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf m|=r,\\qquad |\\mathbf n|=s ,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n\\(r\\) and \\(s\\) being the radii of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively.\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf L(\\lambda)=\\mathbf m+\\lambda(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m),\\qquad\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)\n\n\\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) is the second intersection of \\(\\mathbf L(\\lambda)\\) with\n\\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  Since \\(|\\mathbf L(\\lambda)-\\mathbf m|=r\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{C}= \\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|},\\qquad\n\\mathbf c=\\mathbf C\n      =\\mathbf m+\\frac{r}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}\\,(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{D}= \\frac{s}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|},\\qquad\n\\mathbf d=\\mathbf D\n      =\\mathbf m+\\frac{s}{|\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m|}\\,(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 1  \n\n\\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathbf p|=|\\mathbf p-\\mathbf c|=|\\mathbf p-\\mathbf d|.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(|\\mathbf p|^{2}=|\\mathbf p-\\mathbf c|^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf c=\\frac{|\\mathbf c|^{2}}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf d=\\frac{|\\mathbf d|^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (3)–(4) of \\(\\mathbf c,\\mathbf d\\) into (6) and use\n\\(|\\mathbf m|=r,\\;|\\mathbf n|=s\\).  After a straightforward elimination of the\nunknown scalar product \\(\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m=\\frac{|\\mathbf m|^{2}-rs}{2}},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n=\\frac{|\\mathbf n|^{2}-rs}{2}} .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese are the correct relations (the equalities \\(\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf\nm=|\\mathbf m|^{2}/2\\) and \\(\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n=|\\mathbf n|^{2}/2\\) are\nfalse in general).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is \\(\\{t\\mathbf p\\mid t\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\).\nA point of this line belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n|t\\mathbf p-\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(|\\mathbf m|^{2}=r^{2}\\) (because \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\)) the quadratic\nreduces to  \n\n\\[\nt\\bigl(t|\\mathbf p|^{2}-2\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m\\bigr)=0 .\n\\]\n\nHence the second intersection is obtained for  \n\n\\[\nt_E=\\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf e=\\lambda_E\\mathbf p,\\qquad \n\\lambda_E=\\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same computation with the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf f=\\lambda_F\\mathbf p,\\qquad \n\\lambda_F=\\frac{2\\,\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n}{|\\mathbf p|^{2}} .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf h\\) be the vector of the orthocentre \\(H\\).\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf h-\\mathbf p)\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf n-\\mathbf m)=0 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is perpendicular to \\(PN\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf h-\\mathbf m)\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf p-\\mathbf n)=0 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nExpanding (10)–(11) and eliminating the unknown scalar products\n\\(\\mathbf h\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m\\) and \\(\\mathbf h\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n\\) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf h\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n)\\; } .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Centre \\(U\\) of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathbf u\\) the vector of the centre \\(U\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\).\nBecause \\(|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf e|=|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf f|\\),\n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf e)\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf e-\\mathbf f)=\n\\frac{|\\mathbf e|^{2}-|\\mathbf f|^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nUsing (8)–(9) and the fact that \\(|\\mathbf e|=\\lambda_E|\\mathbf p|\\),\n\\(|\\mathbf f|=\\lambda_F|\\mathbf p|\\) we obtain after simplification\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf u\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=\n        \\frac{\\lambda_E+\\lambda_F}{2}\\,|\\mathbf p|^{2}\\; } .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the expressions of \\(\\lambda_E,\\lambda_F\\) from (8)–(9) and then\nemploying Lemma 1 (7) gives\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf u\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p\n   =\\frac{2\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf m+2\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf n}{2}\n   =\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n) .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\mathbf u\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=\\mathbf p\\!\\cdot\\!(\\mathbf m+\\mathbf n)\\; } .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency\n\nSubtracting (14) from (12) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h)\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf p=0 .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThus the vector \\(\\mathbf u-\\mathbf h\\) (the radius \\(UH\\) of the circle\n\\((BEF)\\)) is orthogonal to the direction \\(\\mathbf p\\) of the line \\(AP\\).\nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular\nto the radius \\(UH\\); a line through a point of a circle that is perpendicular\nto the radius at that point is a tangent.  Therefore the required line is\ntangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for ac