a}\\) also sends the intersection\n   \\(C'\\) of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) with \\(\\omega\\) to the intersection\n   \\(D'\\) of \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) with \\(\\omega\\).  Consequently the\n   similarity sends the second intersection \\(E'\\) of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) with\n   the line \\(AP\\) to the second intersection \\(F'\\) of \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)\n   with the same line.  Hence the quadrilateral \\(C'E'D'F'\\) is cyclic and\n   the triangles \\(AC'D'\\) and \\(AE'F'\\) are directly similar.\n\n3. **Images of the important points.**  \n   Let \\(P'\\) be the image of the circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n   Because the inversion sends circles to circles and preserves\n   incidence, \\(P'\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle AE'F'\\); therefore\n   \\(AP'\\) is the common line of the two circum‑centres and\n   \\(AP'\\perp E'F'\\).  \n   Let \\(H'\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle P'MN\\) (the images of\n   \\(P,M,N\\)).  In a triangle the reflection of the orthocentre in any\n   side lies on the circum‑circle; reflecting \\(H'\\) in the side \\(MN\\) we\n   obtain a point \\(H''\\) on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle P'MN\\).\n\n4. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   Because \\(AP'\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(E'F'\\), the line\n   through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(AP'\\) is perpendicular to the radius\n   \\(P'E'\\) of the circle \\((AE'F')\\).  Hence this line is tangent to the\n   circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BE'F'\\) (the circle \\((BE'F')\\) coincides\n   with \\((AE'F')\\) because \\(B'\\) lies on both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and\n   \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).  Consequently the line through \\(H'\\) parallel to\n   \\(AP'\\) touches the circle \\((BE'F')\\) at a single point.\n\n5. **Undo the inversion.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency: a line tangent to a circle is mapped to a\n   line tangent to the inverse circle.  Therefore, after applying the\n   inverse of \\(\\mathcal I\\), the line through the original ortho