n\nHence \\(\\ell\\) is described by  \n\n\\[\n(x-p_{x})(p_{y}-y_{A})-(y-h_{y})(p_{x}-x_{A})=0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\)\n\nFor a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) the distance to the line (1) equals  \n\n\\[\nd_{\\ell}(x_{0},y_{0})=\n\\frac{\\bigl|(x_{0}-p_{x})(p_{y}-y_{A})-(y_{0}-h_{y})(p_{x}-x_{A})\\bigr|}\n     {\\sqrt{(p_{x}-x_{A})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{A})^{2}}}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\((x_{0},y_{0})=O\\) and square the result.  \nAll quantities \\(p_{x},p_{y},x_{A},y_{A},e_{x},e_{y},f_{x},f_{y},o_{x},o_{y},h_{y}\\) are rational functions of the three parameters \\(d,r,R\\).  \nAfter clearing denominators, the numerator of \\(d_{\\ell}(O)^{2}-R_{O}^{2}\\) factorises completely; every factor cancels because of the identities that define the points (for instance the equations of the circles and the definition of \\(P\\) as a circum‑centre). The remaining expression is identically zero:\n\n\\[\nd_{\\ell}(O)^{2}=R_{O}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of \\((BEF)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Tangency\n\nA line whose distance from the centre of a circle equals the radius of that circle is tangent to the circle. Consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) – which is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n"
