t the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe proof is carried out in a coordinate system; after a straightforward but careful computation one shows that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is at a distance from the circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) equal to the radius of \\((BEF)\\). Hence that line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Place the configuration in a Cartesian frame.**  \n   Take the line \\(MN\\) as the \\(x\\)-axis, put  \n   \\[\n   M=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad r=|MC|,\\qquad R=|ND|\n   \\]\n   with \\(0<r<R\\) and \\(d>0\\).  \n   The two circles intersect at  \n   \\[\n   A=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\; \n          \\sqrt{r^{2}-\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}\\Bigr)^{2}}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   B=\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\;\n          -\\sqrt{r^{2}-\\Bigl(\\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}\\Bigr)^{2}}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the auxiliary points.**  \n   \\[\n   C=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n   \\]  \n   The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(CD\\) (a vertical line) and of \\(AC\\); an elementary computation gives  \n   \\[\n   P=\\Bigl(\\frac{d+R-r