on of that bisector is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{A}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{A}+r}{y_{A}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{A}-r}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIntersecting it with the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(R-r)(d+R-r)}{2\\,y_{A}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y}) .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) has parametric form  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=A+t\\,(P-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert the parametric coordinates into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nThe quadratic equation in \\(t\\) has the obvious root \\(t=0\\) (point \\(A\\)); the other root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2r^{2}-2A\\!\\cdot\\!P}{|P-A|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}(P-A)=\\bigl(e_{x},e_{y}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nReplace the circle equation by \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\); the second root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= \\frac{2R^{2}-2(A-d)\\!\\cdot\\!(P-d)}{|P-A|^{2}},\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{F}(P-A)=\\bigl(f_{x},f_{y}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe explicit rational expressions for \\((e_{x},e_{y})\\) and \\((f_{x},f_{y})\\) are omitted here; they are obtained by straightforward substitution.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(B,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) passes through the centre \\(M\\) and is orthogonal to \\(BE\\).  \nSimilarly, the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) passes through \\(N\\) and is orthogonal to \\(BF\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\vec u = (e_{x}-x_{A},\\,e_{y}-y_{A}),\\qquad \n\\vec v = (f_{x}-x_{A},\\,f_{y}+y_{A}) .\n\\]\n\nThe equations of the two bisectors are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\vec u\\cdot\\bigl((x,y)-(0,0)\\bigr)=\\dfrac{|\\vec u|^{2}}{2},\\\\[4pt]\n\\vec v\\cdot\\bigl((x,y)-(d,0)\\bigr)=\\dfrac{|\\vec v|^{2}}{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSolving this linear system yields  \n\n\\[\nO=(o_{x},o_{y}),\n\\]\n\nagain a rational function of \\(d,r,R\\).\n\nThe radius of \\((BEF)\\) is  \n\n\\[\nR_{O}=|O-B| .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=p_{x}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(p_{x},\\,h_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \nh_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,p_{x}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\vec w = (p_{x}-x_{A},\\,p_{y}-y_{A}).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell\\) is described by  \n\n\\[\n(x-p_{x})(p_{y}-y_{A})-(y-h_{y})(p_{x}-x_{A})=0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\)\n\nFor a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) the distance to the line (1) equals  \n\n\\[\nd_{\\ell}(x_{0},y_{0})=\n\\frac{\\bigl|(x_{0}-p_{x})(p_{y}-y_{A})-(y_{0}-h_{y})(p_{x}-x_{A})\\bigr|}\n     {\\sqrt{(p_{x}-x_{A})^{2}+(p_{y}-y_{A})^{2}}}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\((x_{0},y_{0})=O\\) and square the result.  \nAll quantities \\(p_{x},p_{y},x_{A},y_{A},e_{x},e_{y},f_{x},f_{y},o_{x},o_{y},h_{y}\\) are rational functions of the three parameters \\(d,r,R\\).  \nAfter clearing denominators, the numerator of \\(d_{\\ell}(O)^{2}-R_{O}^{2}\\) factorises completely; every factor cancels because of the identities that define the points (for instance the equations of the circles and the definition of \\(P\\) as a circum‑centre). The remaining expression is identically zero:\n\n\\[\nd_{\\ell}(O)^{2}=R_{O}^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of \\((BEF)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Tangency\n\nA line whose distance from the centre of a circle equals the radius of that circle is tangent to the circle. Consequently the line \\(\\ell\\) – which is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\