ine through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Because of (6) the distance from the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius \\(R\\) of \\((BEF)\\); equivalently,\n   \\[\n   OH=R\\quad\\text{and}\\quad OH\\perp\\ell .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the two given circles be \\(\\Omega\\) (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)), with \\(r<R\\).  \nThey intersect at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nThe line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) (the point nearer to \\(M\\)) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) (the point farther from \\(N\\)).  \nDenote by \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); thus\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\; (=\\,\\rho).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The spiral similarity at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect, there exists a unique spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal S\\) fixes the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), its scale factor must be \\(1\\); otherwise the distance \\(AB\\) would be multiplied by the factor, contradicting the fact that both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) belong to the image of \\(\\Omega\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal S\\) is a pure rotation about \\(A\\).  \nConsequences are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AE=AF}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\boxed{\\angle EAB=\\angle BAF}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, \\(A\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inversion about the circle \\((AP)\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(\\rho^{2}=AP^{2}\\).  \nBecause of (7) the circle \\((AP)\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal I\\) fixes the points \\(A,P,B\\).  \nMoreover \\(\\mathcal I\\) interchanges the two given circles:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Gamma,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Omega .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence the images of the distinguished points are\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal I(D)=C,\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal I(F)=E .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(M'=\\mathcal I(M)\\) and \\(N'=\\mathcal I(N)\\).  \nSince \\(M\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(N\\) on \\(\\Gamma\\), the points \\(M',N'\\) lie respectively on \\(\\Gamma\\) and \\(\\Omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of the orthocenter\n\nThe three sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the lines \\(PM,\\;PN,\\;MN\\).  \nUnder \\(\\mathcal I\\) they are sent to the lines \\(PM',\\;PN',\\;M'N'\\) (the image of a line through the centre of inversion is itself).  \nInversion preserves oriented angles; therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) is mapped onto the line through \\(M'\\) perpendicular to \\(PN'\\), i.e. the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(PN'\\).  \nThe same holds for the other two altitudes.  \nConsequently the image of the orthocenter \\(H\\) is the common intersection of the three perpendicular bisectors of \\(\\triangle PM'N'\\); that is,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal I(H)=O_{1}},\\qquad O_{1}\\text{ is the circumcenter of }\\triangle PM'N'.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal I\\) swaps the circles while fixing the point \\(B\\), the triangle \\(BEF\\) is mapped onto the triangle \\(BFD\\).  \nThe latter triangle lies on the circle \\(\\Gamma\\); its centre is precisely the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nTherefore the circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) is the image of \\(N\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O=\\mathcal I(N)} .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthogonality of \\(HO\\) and \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (11) and (12) we have the four points \\(H,O,N,O_{1}\\) related by the involution \\(\\mathcal I\\):\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(H)=O_{1},\\qquad \\mathcal I(N)=O .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal I\\) is an inversion in the circle \\((AP)\\), the image of a line is a circle orthogonal to \\((AP)\\) and vice‑versa.  \nThe line \\(NO_{1}\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(PN'\\) (by definition of \\(O_{1}\\)), hence it is perpendicular to \\(PN\\).  \nBecause \\(PN\\) passes through the centre \\(P\\) of the inversion, the image of a line through \\(P\\) is itself; therefore the image of the perpendicular line \\(NO_{1}\\) is the line through the images of its endpoints, i.e. the line \\(HO\\).  \nThus \\(HO\\) is the image of a line perpendicular to \\(PN\\); consequently \\(HO\\) is perpendicular to the image of \\(PN\\), which is the line \\(AP\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{HO\\perp AP}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nFrom (13) we have \\(HO\\perp \\ell\\).  \nMoreover, because \\(O\\) is the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\), the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius \\(R\\) of that circle (the radius is exactly the length of the perpendicular from the centre to a tangent).  \nTherefore \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) 