ircle and is therefore fixed:\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(B)=B .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, each is sent to a line:\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe two lines intersect at the fixed point \\(B\\).\n\nDenote the images of the distinguished points by\n\\[\nC'=\\mathcal I(C),\\; D'=\\mathcal I(D),\\; \nE'=\\mathcal I(E),\\; F'=\\mathcal I(F),\\;\nM'=\\mathcal I(M),\\; N'=\\mathcal I(N),\\;\nP'=\\mathcal I(P),\\;\nH'=\\mathcal I(H).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) we have\n\\[\nC',E'\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D',F'\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Geometry of the image configuration\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the point \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\).  \nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) a circle through the centre of inversion becomes a line; consequently the image of \\((ACD)\\) is the line through the images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), i.e. the line \\(C'D'\\).  \nSince \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), its image \\(P'\\) lies on the *perpendicular* to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) through \\(C'\\) and also on the perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) through \\(D'\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nP' = \\ell_{\\Omega}^{\\perp}(C')\\cap \\ell_{\\Gamma}^{\\perp}(D').\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, the three lines \\(PM,PN,MN\\) are transformed into the three lines\n\\[\nPM',\\; PN',\\; M'N'.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nThe altitudes of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the lines through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\), through \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\), and through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause inversion preserves oriented angles, each altitude is sent to the perpendicular bisector of the corresponding side of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\).  \nTherefore the three perpendicular bisectors of \\(\\triangle P'M'N'\\) concur at the image of the orthocentre:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H' = O_{1}},\\qquad \nO_{1}\\text{ is the circumcentre of }\\triangle P'M'N'.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Circumcentre of the image of \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe triangle \\(BEF\\) is mapped by \\(\\mathcal I\\) to the triangle \\(B\\,F'\\,E'\\) (by (1)–(4)).  \nAll three vertices of \\(B\\,F'\\,E'\\) lie on the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); consequently the circumcentre of this triangle is the intersection of the two perpendiculars to those lines through the points \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\).  \nFrom (5) those two perpendiculars are precisely the lines \\(AP\\) (which is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\)) and the line through the image of the centre \\(N\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O = \\mathcal I(N)},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nwhere \\(O\\) denotes the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Orthogonality of \\(HO\\) and \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (7) and (8) we have the four points\n\\[\nH,\\;O,\\;N,\\;O_{1}\n\\]\npaired by the involution \\(\\mathcal I\\):\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(H)=O_{1},\\qquad \\mathcal I(N)=O .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal I\\) is an inversion in the circle \\((AP)\\), the image of any line through the centre \\(P\\) of the inversion is the same line.  \nThe line \\(NO_{1}\\) is the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(P'M'\\) (by definition of \\(O_{1}\\)), hence it is **perpendicular** to the line \\(P'N'\\).  \nSince \\(P'N'\\) is the image of the line \\(PN\\) (which passes through the centre \\(P\\) of the inversion), the image of the perpendicular line \\(NO_{1}\\) is the line \\(HO\\).  \nConsequently \\(HO\\) is the image of a line perpendicular to \\(PN\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{HO\\perp AP}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nFrom (10) we have \\(HO\\perp\\ell\\).  \nSince \\(O\\) is the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\), the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\) equals the radius of that circle; equivalently,\n\\[\nOH = \\text{radius of }(BEF)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad OH\\perp\\ell .\n\\]\nThus \\(\\ell\\) touches the circle \\((BEF)\\) at exactly one point; i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nHence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎"
