})}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr] .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nGroup the fractions with the same denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n&=d\\Bigl[\n\\frac{x_{A}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{A}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{A}+d}\n-\n\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{P}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nObserve that  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{A}(y_{P}-y_{A})\n= x_{A}x_{P}-x_{A}^{2}-y_{A}y_{P}+y_{A}^{2}\n= (x_{A}^{2}+y_{A}^{2})-(x_{A}x_{P}+y_{A}y_{P}).\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nUsing the circle equations (1) we have \\(x_{A}^{2}+y_{A}^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nSimilarly, from the definition of \\(P\\) (equal distances to \\(A\\) and \\(C\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}x_{P}+y_{A}y_{P}= \\frac{r^{2}+x_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (22)–(23) into the first fraction of (21) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{A}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{A}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{A}+d}\n=\n\\frac{r^{2}-\\dfrac{r^{2}+x_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}}{2}}{x_{A}+d}\n=\n\\frac{r^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}}{2\\,(x_{A}+d)} .\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nA completely analogous computation for the second fraction (using the fact that \\(PD=PA\\) and that \\(D\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis at \\(x=d+R\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{P}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{P}+d}\n=\n\\frac{R^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}}{2\\,(x_{P}+d)} .\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nInsert (24) and (25) into (21):\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n=\n\\frac{d}{2}\\Bigl[\n\\frac{r^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}}{x_{A}+d}\n-\n\\frac{R^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr].\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nNow use the explicit expression (6) for \\(x_{P}\\) together with (7) for \\(y_{P}\\); a short substitution shows that  \n\n\\[\nx_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}= \\frac{r^{2}+R^{2}+d^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}= -\\frac{R^{2}+d^{2}}{2},\n\\qquad\nR^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}= -\\frac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nPlugging (28) into (26) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n=\n\\frac{d}{2}\\Bigl[\n\\frac{-\\dfrac{R^{2}+d^{2}}{2}}{x_{A}+d}\n-\n\\frac{-\\dfrac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{2}}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr]\n=\n\\frac{d}{4}\\Bigl[\n\\frac{-(R^{2}+d^{2})}{x_{A}+d}\n+\\frac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr].\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\n\nBut from (2) and (6) we have  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}+d = \\frac{R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\n\\qquad\nx_{P}+d = \\frac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}.\n\\tag{30}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (30) into (29) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n=\n\\frac{d}{4}\\Bigl[\n\\frac{-(R^{2}+d^{2})}{\\dfrac{R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}}\n+\\frac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{\\dfrac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}}\n\\Bigr]\n=\n\\frac{d}{4}\\bigl[-2d+2d\\bigr]=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\overrightarrow{HO}\\perp\\overrightarrow{AP}} .\n\\tag{31}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nEquation (31) tells us that the segment \\(HO\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circle \\((BEF)\\) (a radius is perpendicular to a tangent at the point of tangency).  \n\nHence the required statement is proved. ∎"
