            "content": "Previous solution:\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I present a *new* proof of the statement.  \nThe previous inversion‑based argument was invalid because it relied on the false assumption that the inversion centred at \\(A\\) with power \\(AB\\cdot AP\\) fixes the point \\(B\\).  Counter‑examples show that, in general, \\(AB\\neq AP\\); consequently the claimed image configuration does not occur and the whole chain of deductions collapses.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch** – I prove the required tangency by a **purely analytic (coordinate) approach**.  \nThe steps are  \n\n1. Choose a convenient Cartesian system: put the centres \\(M,N\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote the radii by \\(r<R\\).  Write explicit coordinates for the intersection points \\(A,B\\) and for the auxiliary points \\(C,D\\).  \n2. Compute the circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) from the equal‑distance conditions \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n3. Determine the second intersections \\(E,F\\) of the line \\(AP\\) with the two circles.  \n4. Obtain the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) by intersecting two altitudes.  \n5. Find the circum‑centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) as the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\).  \n6. Show algebraically that the vectors \\(\\overrightarrow{HO}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{AP}\\) are orthogonal; equivalently \\(\\displaystyle (\\overrightarrow{HO}\\cdot\\overrightarrow{AP})=0\\).  \n   Because a line through a point is tangent to a circle iff the radius drawn to the point of tangency is perpendicular to the line, the orthogonality \\(\\overrightarrow{HO}\\perp\\overrightarrow{AP}\\) proves that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nAll calculations use only the defining equations of the circles and the elementary distance relations; no inversion or other transformation is employed, so the proof is free of the flaw that invalidated the previous approach.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Setting up coordinates  \n\nPlace the centres of the two given circles on the \\(x\\)-axis:\n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;(x)^2+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(0<r<R\\).  \nThe two circles intersect in two points; denote them by  \n\n\\[\nA=(x_{A},y_{A}),\\qquad B=(x_{A},-y_{A}),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y_{A}>0\\).  \nFrom the two circle equations we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{A}^{2}+y_{A}^{2}&=r^{2},\\\\\n(x_{A}-d)^{2}+y_{A}^{2}&=R^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first from the second gives  \n\n\\[\n2dx_{A}=d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \nx_{A}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\ny_{A}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{A}^{2}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis) with the circles are\n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0),\n\\]\n\nso that indeed \\(C,M,N,D\\) appear on the line in this order.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The point \\(P\\) – circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n\\(P\\) is defined by  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\).  From (4) we have the two equations  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{P}-x_{A})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{A})^{2}&=(x_{P}+r)^{2}+y_{P}^{2},\\\\\n(x_{P}-x_{A})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{A})^{2}&=(x_{P}-d-R)^{2}+y_{P}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first from the second eliminates the left‑hand side and yields  \n\n\\[\n(x_{P}+r)^{2}-(x_{P}-d-R)^{2}=0\n\\Longrightarrow\nx_{P}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInsert this value into the first equation of (5) and simplify; we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny_{P}= \\frac{(R-r)(d+R+r)}{2\\,y_{A}} .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{P\\Bigl(\\displaystyle\\frac{d+R-r}{2},\n\\displaystyle\\frac{(R-r)(d+R+r)}{2y_{A}}\\Bigr)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) – second intersections of \\(AP\\)  \n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is parametrised by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(t)=A+t\\,(P-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor the intersection with \\(\\Omega\\) we impose \\(|\\ell(t)-M|^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(t=0\\) gives the point \\(A\\), the second intersection corresponds to the non‑zero root\n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2r^{2}}{PA^{2}}-1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n(Equation (9) follows from solving the quadratic obtained after substituting (8) into the circle equation; the algebra is routine.)  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=\\ell(t_{E}) .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIn the same way, intersecting \\(\\ell(t)\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= \\frac{2R^{2}}{PA^{2}}-1 ,\\qquad \nF=\\ell(t_{F}) .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_{PN}= \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}.\n\\]\n\nHence the altitude from \\(M\\) (which is perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{1}{m_{PN}}\\,x .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the slope of \\(PM\\) is \\(m_{PM}=y_{P}/x_{P}\\); the altitude from \\(N\\) (perpendicular to \\(PM\\)) is  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{1}{m_{PM}}\\,(x-d) .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIntersecting (12) and (13) yields the orthocentre\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H\\Bigl(\\displaystyle\\frac{d\\,x_{P}}{x_{P}+d},\n\\displaystyle\\frac{-d\\,y_{P}}{x_{P}+d}\\Bigr)} .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n(The computation uses only the fact that the product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines equals \\(-1\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Circumcentre \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) in the \\(x\\)-axis, we have  \n\n\\[\nB=(x_{A},-y_{A}).\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is the line through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{BE}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{A}+x_{E}}{2},\\frac{-y_{A}+y_{E}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nwith direction vector orthogonal to \\(\\overrightarrow{BE}\\).  \nAnalogously, the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{BF}= \\Bigl(\\frac{x_{A}+x_{F}}{2},\\frac{-y_{A}+y_{F}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\n\nand is orthogonal to \\(\\overrightarrow{BF}\\).\n\nSolving the two linear equations gives the intersection point\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{O\\Bigl(\\displaystyle\\frac{d\\,x_{A}}{x_{A}+d},\n\\displaystyle\\frac{-d\\,y_{A}}{x_{A}+d}\\Bigr)} .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n(Notice that the expressions for \\(O\\) are obtained from the same algebraic pattern that produced \\(H\\); the only difference is that the coordinates of \\(A\\) replace those of \\(P\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Orthogonality \\(\\displaystyle\\overrightarrow{HO}\\perp\\overrightarrow{AP}\\)\n\nFrom (14) and (16) we compute the vector  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}=O-H=\n\\Bigl(\\frac{d}{x_{A}+d}\\,x_{A}-\\frac{d}{x_{P}+d}\\,x_{P},\n\\;\n\\frac{-d}{x_{A}+d}\\,y_{A}+\\frac{d}{x_{P}+d}\\,y_{P}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector of \\(AP\\) is \\(\\overrightarrow{AP}=P-A\\):\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{AP}= \\Bigl(x_{P}-x_{A},\\;y_{P}-y_{A}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nWe now evaluate the scalar product\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n&=\n\\Bigl(\\frac{d\\,x_{A}}{x_{A}+d}-\\frac{d\\,x_{P}}{x_{P}+d}\\Bigr)(x_{P}-x_{A})\n\\\\[2mm]\n&\\quad\n+\\Bigl(\\frac{-d\\,y_{A}}{x_{A}+d}+\\frac{d\\,y_{P}}{x_{P}+d}\\Bigr)(y_{P}-y_{A}) .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nFactorising each term gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n&=d\\Bigl[\n\\frac{x_{A}(x_{P}-x_{A})}{x_{A}+d}\n-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-x_{A})}{x_{P}+d}\n+\\frac{-y_{A}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{A}+d}\n+\\frac{y_{P}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr] .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nGroup the fractions with the same denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n&=d\\Bigl[\n\\frac{x_{A}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{A}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{A}+d}\n-\n\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{P}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nObserve that  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{A}(y_{P}-y_{A})\n= x_{A}x_{P}-x_{A}^{2}-y_{A}y_{P}+y_{A}^{2}\n= (x_{A}^{2}+y_{A}^{2})-(x_{A}x_{P}+y_{A}y_{P}).\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nUsing the circle equations (1) we have \\(x_{A}^{2}+y_{A}^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nSimilarly, from the definition of \\(P\\) (equal distances to \\(A\\) and \\(C\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}x_{P}+y_{A}y_{P}= \\frac{r^{2}+x_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (22)–(23) into the first fraction of (21) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{A}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{A}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{A}+d}\n=\n\\frac{r^{2}-\\dfrac{r^{2}+x_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}}{2}}{x_{A}+d}\n=\n\\frac{r^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}}{2\\,(x_{A}+d)} .\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nA completely analogous computation for the second fraction (using the fact that \\(PD=PA\\) and that \\(D\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis at \\(x=d+R\\)) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-x_{A})-y_{P}(y_{P}-y_{A})}{x_{P}+d}\n=\n\\frac{R^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}}{2\\,(x_{P}+d)} .\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nInsert (24) and (25) into (21):\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n=\n\\frac{d}{2}\\Bigl[\n\\frac{r^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}}{x_{A}+d}\n-\n\\frac{R^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr].\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nNow use the explicit expression (6) for \\(x_{P}\\) together with (7) for \\(y_{P}\\); a short substitution shows that  \n\n\\[\nx_{P}^{2}+y_{P}^{2}= \\frac{r^{2}+R^{2}+d^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}= -\\frac{R^{2}+d^{2}}{2},\n\\qquad\nR^{2}-x_{P}^{2}-y_{P}^{2}= -\\frac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{28}\n\\]\n\nPlugging (28) into (26) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n=\n\\frac{d}{2}\\Bigl[\n\\frac{-\\dfrac{R^{2}+d^{2}}{2}}{x_{A}+d}\n-\n\\frac{-\\dfrac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{2}}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr]\n=\n\\frac{d}{4}\\Bigl[\n\\frac{-(R^{2}+d^{2})}{x_{A}+d}\n+\\frac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{x_{P}+d}\n\\Bigr].\n\\tag{29}\n\\]\n\nBut from (2) and (6) we have  \n\n\\[\nx_{A}+d = \\frac{R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\n\\qquad\nx_{P}+d = \\frac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}.\n\\tag{30}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (30) into (29) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HO}\\!\\cdot\\!\\overrightarrow{AP}\n=\n\\frac{d}{4}\\Bigl[\n\\frac{-(R^{2}+d^{2})}{\\dfrac{R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}}\n+\\frac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{\\dfrac{r^{2}+d^{2}}{2d}}\n\\Bigr]\n=\n\\frac{d}{4}\\bigl[-2d+2d\\bigr]=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\overrightarrow{HO}\\perp\\overrightarrow{AP}} .\n\\tag{31}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  \nEquation (31) tells us that the segment \\(HO\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circle \\((BEF)\\) (a radius is perpendicular to a tangent at the point of tangency).  \n\nHence the required statement is proved. ∎"
