cum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); consequently the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\) is the line through \\(P\\) orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the point \\(P\\) belongs to that perpendicular bisector, whence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore the line \\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) also satisfies  \n\n\\[\nL\\perp MN .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,L\\) is cyclic\n\nConsider the reflections of the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) in the line \\(MN\\).  \nSince \\(h\\) fixes the line \\(AB\\) (the radical axis of the two circles) and sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) under reflection in \\(MN\\) are exchanged:\n\n\\[\n\\text{ref}_{MN}(E)=F,\\qquad \\text{ref}_{MN}(F)=E .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the segment \\(EF\\) is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\); its midpoint lies on \\(MN\\).  \n\nBecause \\(L\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\) (by (4)), the right angle  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BLE = 90^{\\circ} \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nholds: the line \\(BL\\) is the image of \\(BE\\) after a rotation of \\(90^{\\circ}\\) about the midpoint of \\(EF\\), which lies on \\(MN\\).  \n\nNow \\(B,E,F\\) are three distinct points, and (6) shows that the quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,L\\) has a pair of opposite angles summing to \\(180^{\\circ}\\); therefore it is cyclic:\n\n\\[\nB,E,F,L\\ \\text{lie on a common circle}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Tangency condition\n\nFrom the cyclicity (7) we obtain the equality of the angles subtended by the same chord:\n\n\\[\n\\angle (L,BE)=\\angle BFE .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L\\parallel AP\\) and the points \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear, the left‑hand side of (8) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (AP,BE)