ag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inversion with centre \\(A\\)\n\nDefine the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\n\\rho^{2}=R_{\\Omega}\\,R_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\rho^{2}=R_{\\Omega}R_{\\Gamma}\\), the two circles are mutually\ninverse:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Gamma ,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Omega .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the images of the points listed in (1) are exactly the points\non the opposite circle:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal I(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal I(M)=N .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The point \\(P\\) is fixed by the inversion\n\n\\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and fixes \\(A\\), the circle\n\\((ACD)\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  Its centre therefore is\nfixed:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(P)=P .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Behaviour of the orthocentre under the inversion\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  By (3) the inversion\nmaps the triangle \\(PMN\\) onto the triangle \\(P N M\\) (the vertices\n\\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are interchanged while \\(P\\) stays fixed).  Orthocentres\nare preserved by an inversion: the image of the orthocentre of a triangle\nis the orthocentre of the image triangle.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(H)=H',\\qquad\\text{where }H'\\text{ is the orthocentre of }\n\\triangle P N M .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The line \\(\\ell\\) and its image\n\nDenote by \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Because\n\\(A\\) is the centre of the inversion, the image of a line through \\(A\\)\nis the same line; a line **parallel** to a given line is transformed into\na line *