he circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(A\\) (because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\)), hence the hypothesis is satisfied and we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   H^{*}=O_{\\,PN^{*}M^{*}},\\qquad\\text{the circum‑centre of }\\triangle PN^{*}M^{*}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The circle \\((BEF)\\) is invariant.**  \n   The set \\(\\{B,E,F\\}\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\) (both \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are interchanged, \\(B\\) is fixed).  \n   Therefore the circum‑circle \\(\\omega:=(BEF)\\) is invariant: \\(\\mathcal I(\\omega)=\\omega\\).\n\n6. **The image of the required line.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) does **not** pass through the centre of inversion, its image \\(\\ell^{*}=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\) and \\(H^{*}\\).  \n   Moreover, a line parallel to a given line is transformed by an inversion into a circle through the centre of inversion that is **orthogonal** to the image of the given line.  \n   Since the image of the line \\(AP\\) is itself (it passes through the centre of inversion), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell^{*}\\text{ is the circle through }A\\text{ and }H^{*}\\text{ orthogonal to }AP. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Orthogonality gives tangency.**  \n   From (1) we know that \\(H^{*}\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PN^{*}M^{*}\\); consequently \\(AH^{*}\\) is a diameter of the invariant circle \\(\\omega\\).  \n   Indeed, the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two original circles, hence after inversion they become the second intersections of the same line \\(AP\\) with the swapped circles; therefore \\(A,E,F\\) are collinear and \\(AE\\cdot AF=R_{\\Omega}R_{\\Gamma}= \\rho^{2}\\), i.e. \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  \n   Hence the radius of \\(\\omega\\) through the point \\(H^{*}\\) is precisely the line \\(AH^{*}\\).  \n\n   Because \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is orthogonal to \\(AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is the line through the centre \\(A\\) and the radius \\(AH^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\), the circle \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at the point \\(H^{*}\\).\n\n8. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   Tangency is preserved by inversion.  Since \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(H^{*}\\) and \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps \\(\\ell\\) to \\(\\ell^{*}\\) while fixing \\(\\omega\\), the original line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\) as well.\n\nThus the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii  \n  \\(R_{\\Omega}<R_{\\Gamma}\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles,\n  ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with\n  \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);  \n* \\(\\ell\\) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\);  \n* \\(\\omega\\) the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nWe shall prove that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion at \\(A\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\n\\rho^{2}=R_{\\Omega}R_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the power equals the product of the radii, the two circles are\nmutually inverse:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\Gamma ,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\Omega . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(C)=D,\\qquad \\mathcal I(E)=F,\\qquad \\mathcal I(B)=B . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the circle \\((ACD)\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\);\nits centre \\(P\\) is therefore fixed:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(P)=P . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Images of the centres \\(M,N\\)\n\nBecause \\(A\\) lies on both circles, we have  \n\n\\[\nAM=R_{\\Omega},\\qquad AN=R_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\(X\\) not on the circle of inversion, its image \\(X^{*}\\) lies on the\nray \\(AX\\) and satisfies \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}= \\rho^{2}=R_{\\Omega}R_{\\Gamma}\\).\nHence  \n\n\\[\nAM^{*}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}R_{\\Gamma}}{AM}=R_{\\Gamma},\\qquad\nAN^{*}= \\frac{R_{\\Omega}R_{\\Gamma}}{AN}=R_{\\Omega}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(M^{*}\\) lies on the ray \\(AM\\) at distance \\(R_{\\Gamma}\\) from \\(A\\),\nand \\(N^{*}\\) lies on the ray \\(AN\\) at distance \\(R_{\\Omega}\\) from \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Image of the orthocentre\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and set \\(H^{*}= \\mathcal I(H)\\).\nThe inversion sends the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) to \\(\\triangle PN^{*}M^{*}\\)\n(because \\(P\\) is fixed, while \\(M,N\\) are sent to \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) respectively).\n\nA well‑known property of an inversion whose centre lies on the circum‑circle\nof a triangle (see e.g. Johnson, *Advanced Euclidean Geometry*, Chap. 9) is:\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal I\\) be an inversion with centre \\(O\\) that maps a triangle\n> \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) to \\(\\triangle X'Y'Z'\\).  \n> If \\(O\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\), then the image of the\n> orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle X'Y'Z'\\).\n\nIn our configuration the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) passes through \\(A\\):\nindeed \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and the points \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), so the\ncircum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(P\\) and the circle \\((PMN)\\) contains\n\\(A\\).  Therefore the hypothesis of the lemma is satisfied and we obtain\n\n\\[\nH^{*}=O_{\\,PN^{*}M^{*}},\\qquad\\text{the circum‑centre of }\\triangle PN^{*}M^{*}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The invariant circle \\(\\omega\\)\n\nFrom (2) the set \\(\\{B,E,F\\}\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\); consequently\nits circum‑circle \\(\\omega:=(BEF)\\) is invariant:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\omega)=\\omega . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Image of the line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\); it does **not**\ncontain the centre \\(A\\) of the inversion.  Hence its image \\(\\ell^{*}=\n\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle passing through \\(A\\) and \\(H^{*}\\).\n\nBecause inversion preserves angles, a line parallel to a given line is\ntransformed into a circle through the centre of inversion that is orthogonal to\nthe image of the given line.  The line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of the\ninversion, therefore it is invariant; consequently\n\n\\[\n\\ell^{*}\\text{ is the circle through }A\\text{ and }H^{*}\\text{ orthogonal to }AP.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Orthogonality yields tangency\n\nFrom (5) the point \\(H^{*}\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PN^{*}M^{*}\\).\nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with\nthe two original circles; after the inversion they remain the second\nintersections of the same line \\(AP\\) with the swapped circles.  Hence the\nchord \\(EF\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) and\n\\(A\\) is the midpoint of that chord.  Therefore the radius of \\(\\omega\\) that\npasses through the midpoint of \\(EF\\) is precisely the line \\(AH^{*}\\); in other\nwords \\(AH^{*}\\) is a radius of \\(\\omega\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is orthogonal to \\(AP\\) and \\(AP\\) contains the centre \\(A\\)\nand the radius \\(AH^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\), the circle \\(\\ell^{*}\\) meets \\(\\omega\\)\nin exactly one point, namely \\(H^{*}\\); thus \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega\\)\nat \\(H^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Back to the original configuration\n\nTangency is preserved by inversion.  Because \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps \\(\\ell\\) to\n\\(\\ell^{*}\\) and fixes the circle \\(\\omega\\), the fact that \\(\\ell^{*}\\) is\ntangent to \\(\\omega\\) implies that the original line \\(\\ell\\) is also tangent\nto \\(\\omega\\).\n\nHence the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) touches the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎"
