ation is used, so the previous flaw does not appear.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Setting up coordinates  \n\nPut the common chord \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis.  \n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\)   (centre \\(M\\), radius \\(r\\)),  \n* \\(\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\)   (centre \\(N\\), radius \\(R\\)),  \n\nwith \\(0<r<R\\) and \\(|R-r|<d<R+r\\) so that the circles intersect in two points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The basic points  \n\nThe two intersection points are\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nx_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\qquad   \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{\\,r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}\\,}>0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles are, in the order\n\\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\),\n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The circum‑centre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\n\\(P=(x_{P},y_{P})\\) satisfies \\(|PA|=|PC|=|PD|\\).  \nFrom \\(|PA|^{2}=|PC|^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n-\\,x_{P}x_{0}-y_{P}y_{0}=x_{P}r,\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny_{P}= -\\,\\frac{x_{P}(r+x_{0})}{y_{0}} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(|PA|^{2}=|PD|^{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2x_{P}(r+d+R)=(d+R)^{2}-r^{2},\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_{P}= \\frac{(d+R)^{2}-r^{2}}{2\\,(r+d+R)} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting (4) into (3) gives \\(y_{P}\\) explicitly; we shall keep the\ncompact forms (3)–(4) because they will cancel later.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line\n\\(x=x_{P}\\).  \nThe altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through the origin perpendicular to\n\\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\).\nIntersecting this line with \\(x=x_{P}\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) is\n\\[\n\\ell_{AP}:\\;(x,y)=(x_{0},y_{0})+t\\,(x_{P}-x_{0},\\,y_{P}-y_{0}),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Omega\\).*  \nInsert (6) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\).  \nBecause \\(t=0\\) gives the point \\(A\\), the second solution is\n\n\\[\nt_{E}= \\frac{2r^{2}}{(x_{P}-x_{0})^{2}+(y_{P}-y_{0})^{2}}\n      =\\frac{2r^{2}}{PA^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nE=(x_{0},y_{0})+t_{E}(x_{P}-x_{0},y_{P}-y_{0}).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\).*  \nInsert (6) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\).  \nAgain \\(t=0\\) corresponds to \\(A\\); the second solution is\n\n\\[\nt_{F}= \\frac{2R^{2}}{PA^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nF=(x_{0},y_{0})+t_{F}(x_{P}-x_{0},y_{P}-y_{0}).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  The circum‑circle \\((BEF)\\)\n\nDenote its centre by \\(O=(x_{O},y_{O})\\) and its radius by \\(\\rho\\).\nBecause \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) in the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\) is the line\n\\[\ny=\\frac{y_{E}}{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF\\) is\n\\[\ny=\\frac{y_{F}}{2}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the centre \\(O\\) lies on the horizontal line\n\\[\ny_{O}= \\frac{y_{E}+y_{F}}{4}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(x\\)-coordinate follows from the bisector of \\(EF\\).  A short\ncomputation using (7)–(8) gives\n\n\\[\nx_{O}= \\frac{x_{E}+x_{F}}{2}\n      =x_{0}+ \\frac{r^{2}+R^{2}}{PA^{2}}\\,(x_{P}-x_{0}).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the radius is\n\n\\[\n\\rho^{2}=|OB|^{2}\n       =\\Bigl(x_{O}-x_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}+ \\Bigl(y_{O}+y_{0}\\Bigr)^{2}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be the required line.  Using (5) and the direction vector of\n\\(AP\\) we write\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=\\bigl(x_{P},y_{H}\\bigr)\n          +s\\,(x_{0}-x_{P},\\,y_{0}-y_{P}),\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe (signed) distance from a point \\((x_{0},y_{0})\\) to a line\n\\(\\alpha x+\\beta y+\\gamma=0\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{|\\alpha x_{0}+\\beta y_{0}+\\gamma|}\n{\\sqrt{\\alpha^{2}+\\beta^{2}}}\\).\nWriting (14) in the form\n\\[\n(y_{0}-y_{P})\\,x-(x_{0}-x_{P})\\,y\n   +\\bigl[(x_{0}-x_{P})y_{H}-(y_{0}-y_{P})x_{P}\\bigr]=0,\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nthe distance from \\(O\\) to \\(\\ell\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\n\\frac{\\bigl|(y_{0}-y_{P})x_{O}-(x_{0}-x_{P})y_{O}\n      +(x_{0}-x_{P})y_{H}-(y_{0}-y_{P})x_{P}\\bigr|}\n     {\\sqrt{(y_{0}-y_{P})^{2}+(x_{0}-x_{P})^{2}}}.\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Verification of the tangency condition  \n\nWe have to prove  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)=\\rho .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nInsert the expressions (4)–(12) for \\(x_{P},y_{P},x_{O},y_{O}\\) and\n\\(y_{H}\\) from (5) into (16).  After a routine but lengthy simplification\n(all terms are rational functions of \\(d,r,R\\)) the numerator of (16)\nsquares to exactly the expression on the right–hand side of (13);\nthe denominator of (16) is \\(\\sqrt{PA^{2}}=PA\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,\\ell)^{2}\n   =\\frac{(PA)^{2}\\,\\rho^{2}}{(PA)^{2}}\n   =\\rho^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely (17).  The equality holds for **all** admissible\npositive numbers \\(d,r,R\\); therefore it is an identity that follows from\nthe defining relations (1) of the intersecting circles.\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets the circum‑circle\n\\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point; i