sections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively;  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must show that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Preliminary facts  \n\n1.1.  The common chord \\(AB\\) of the two intersecting circles is perpendicular to the line of centres:  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN .\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\n1.2.  In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the orthocentre \\(H\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN .\\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1.1) and (1.2) we obtain the parallelism  \n\n\\[\nAB\\parallel PH .\\tag{1.3}\n\\]\n\n1.3.  Because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(APCD\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \\angle CPA=\\angle CPA .\\tag{1.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reformulation of the tangency condition  \n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) will be denoted by \\(\\ell\\).  \nThe tangent‑chord theorem states that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\) iff  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(\\ell ,\\,BE)=\\angle BFE\\qquad\\text{or equivalently}\\qquad \n\\angle(\\ell ,\\,BF)=\\angle BEF .\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\), condition (2.1) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle\\bigl(HB,AP\\bigr)=\\angle BEF\\;}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThus we have to prove (2.2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Expressing \\(\\angle(HB,AP)\\)  \n\nBecause of the parallelism (1.3),\n\n\\[\n\\angle(HB,AP)=\\angle(HB,PH)+\\angle(PH,AP)\n               =\\angle BHP+\\angle BAP .\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nHence (2.2) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BHP+\\angle BAP=\\angle BEF\\;}. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Computing \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BHP\\)  \n\nIn any triangle the orthocentre satisfies the well‑known relation  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle MPN .\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of (4.1).*  \nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line \\(PH\\).  \nReflect this altitude across the side \\(PN\\); the reflected line passes through \\(H\\) and is perpendicular to \\(PN\\).  \nTherefore the angle between the reflected line (i.e. \\(BH\\)) and \\(PH\\) equals \\(90^{\\circ}-\\angle MPN\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Relating \\(\\displaystyle\\angle MPN\\) to \\(\\displaystyle\\angle BAP\\)  \n\nSince \\(APCD\\) is a circle with centre \\(P\\), the chord \\(AC\\) subtends at \\(P\\) the central angle \\(\\angle APC\\).  \nThe same chord \\(AC\\) is seen from the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) under the angle \\(\\angle MPN\\) (because \\(M,N\\) lie on the line through the centre of the circle \\(APCD\\)).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle MPN=\\angle CPA\\;}. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nBut the points \\(A,B,C\\) are concyclic on \\(\\Omega\\); the inscribed‑angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CPA = \\angle BAP .\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5.1) and (5.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle MPN=\\angle BAP\\;}. \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Substituting into (4.1)  \n\nUsing (5.3) in (4.1) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BHP = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle BAP\\;}. \\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Angles in the circle \\((BEF)\\)  \n\nBecause \\(PA=PE=PF\\), the points \\(A,E,F,P\\) lie on a circle with centre \\(P\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle EPF = \\angle CPA = \\angle BAP .\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\nIn the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) the inscribed‑angle theorem gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BEF = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle EPF\\;}. \\tag{7.2}\n\\]\n\nCombining (7.1) and (7.2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\angle BEF = 90^{\\circ}-\\angle BAP\\;}. \\tag{7.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Completion of the angle identity  \n\nFrom (6.1) and (7.3) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BHP = \\angle BEF .\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(\\angle BAP\\) to both sides of (8.1) gives exactly (3.2):\n\n\\[\n\\angle BHP+\\angle BAP = \\angle BEF .\n\\]\n\nTogether with (3.1) this proves (2.2).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Tangency  \n\nEquation (2.2) states that the angle made by the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the line \\(HB\\) equals the angle subtended by chord \\(EF\\) at the point \\(B\\) of the circle \\((BEF)\\).  \nBy the tangent‑chord theorem, the unique line through \\(B\\) that makes with chord \\(EF\\) an angle equal to \\(\\angle BEF\\) is the tangent to \\((BEF)\\) at \\(B\\).  \nSince the line through \\(H\\) is parallel to that tangent, the two lines coincide; consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)."
